update public.levels_partes set "Descripción"='Part 2: Open Cloze`nFor questions 9–16, read the text below and think of the word which best fits each gap. Use only ONE word in each gap. There is an example at the beginning (0).`n`nExample:`n0 → of`n`nText`nThe Popularity of Podcasts`nIn recent years, podcasts have become one (0) ___ the most popular ways to consume information and entertainment. People all over the world listen to them while commuting, exercising, or simply relaxing at home.`nOne reason for their success is the wide range of topics available. There is a podcast for almost every interest, (9) ___ politics and science to comedy and storytelling. This variety makes it easy for listeners to find content that suits their preferences.`nAnother advantage is convenience. Unlike traditional radio, podcasts can be listened to at any time, depending (10) ___ the listener’s schedule. This flexibility has made them particularly appealing to busy individuals.`nIn addition, many podcasts are free, which allows people to access high-quality content without having to pay (11) ___. This has helped increase their popularity even further.`nHowever, producing a successful podcast requires more (12) ___ just a good idea. Creators need to plan their episodes carefully and ensure that the content is engaging enough to keep listeners interested.`nIt is also important to promote the podcast effectively. Without proper marketing, even the best content may go unnoticed (13) ___ the large number of podcasts available today.`nDespite these challenges, many people continue to create podcasts because they enjoy sharing their ideas and connecting (14) ___ others. For some, it even becomes a full-time job.`nOverall, podcasts have changed the way people consume media, and they are likely to remain popular for years to come. As technology continues to improve, their influence is expected to grow even (15) ___.`n`nQuestions`n9 ______10 ______11 ______12 ______13 ______14 ______15 ______16 ______`n`nAnswer Key`n9 from10 on11 anything12 than13 among14 with15 further16 ahead (Nota: este último puede variar según interpretación, pero “ahead” encaja como cierre natural)`n' where id='c7d425b7-63cf-4420-abdc-f38a28111259';
update public.levels_partes set "Descripción"='Part 3: Word Formation`nFor questions 17–24, read the text below. Use the word given in capitals at the end of some of the lines to form a word that fits in the gap in the same line. There is an example at the beginning (0).`n`nExample:`n0 → useful (USE)`n`nText`nThe Advantages of Volunteering`nVolunteering is becoming increasingly popular, especially among young people who are looking for (0) ___ experiences. (USE)`nOne of the main benefits of volunteering is the opportunity for personal (17) ___. It allows individuals to learn new skills and gain confidence. (DEVELOP)`nIn addition, volunteering can improve your (18) ___ skills, as you often need to work with people from different backgrounds. (COMMUNICATE)`nMany organisations depend on the (19) ___ of volunteers to continue their work. Without them, it would be difficult to provide support to those in need. (CONTRIBUTE)`nAnother advantage is that volunteering can be extremely (20) ___. Helping others often brings a sense of satisfaction and purpose. (REWARD)`nMoreover, it can increase your (21) ___ when applying for jobs, as employers value candidates with experience in teamwork and responsibility. (EMPLOY)`nHowever, it is important to choose the right type of volunteering. If the activity is not (22) ___ for you, it may become less enjoyable over time. (SUIT)`nVolunteers must also be (23) ___ and committed, as organisations rely on them to carry out important tasks. (RELY)`nIn conclusion, volunteering is a highly (24) ___ activity that can benefit both individuals and society as a whole. (VALUE)`n`nAnswer Key`n17 development18 communication19 contribution20 rewarding21 employability22 suitable23 reliable24 valuable`n' where id='1c4186d0-fdbe-41e7-8266-efdf712c3006';
update public.levels_partes set "Descripción"='Part 4: Key Word Transformations`nFor questions 25–30, complete the second sentence so that it has a similar meaning to the first sentence, using the word given. Do not change the word given. You must use between two and five words, including the word given. There is an example at the beginning (0).`n`nExample:`n0I last saw him two years ago.seen`nI haven’t __________________ him for two years.`nAnswer: seen`n`nQuestions`n25It was not necessary for me to wake up early yesterday.have`nI __________________ wake up early yesterday.`n`n26“Don’t forget to send the email,” she told me.reminded`nShe __________________ the email.`n`n27This is the best restaurant I have ever been to.never`nI __________________ a better restaurant than this one.`n`n28They are repairing the road outside my house.being`nThe road outside my house __________________ repaired.`n`n29I regret not studying harder for the exam.wish`nI __________________ harder for the exam.`n`n30It is possible that he will arrive late.may`nHe __________________ late.`n`nAnswer Key`n25 didn’t have to26 reminded me to send27 have never been to28 is being29 wish I had studied30 may arrive`n' where id='d02d4a2a-734c-4a46-8c7e-7b95734ee84d';
update public.levels_partes set "Descripción"='Part 5 – Multiple Choice`nYou are going to read a text about learning new skills. For questions 31–36, choose the answer (A, B, C or D) which you think fits best according to the text.`n`nThe Challenge of Learning Something New`nMany people believe that learning new skills becomes increasingly difficult with age. While it is true that children often pick up new abilities more quickly, research suggests that adults have their own advantages when it comes to learning.`nOne reason adults may struggle is that they tend to be more afraid of making mistakes. Unlike children, who often learn through trial and error without worrying about failure, adults can become discouraged if they do not succeed immediately. This fear can slow down the learning process, as it prevents them from experimenting freely.`nHowever, adults usually have better concentration and are more motivated. They often choose to learn skills that are directly relevant to their personal or professional lives. For example, someone might learn a new language to improve their job opportunities or take up cooking to lead a healthier lifestyle. This clear purpose can make learning more effective.`nAnother advantage adults have is their ability to connect new information with existing knowledge. Because they have more life experience, they can relate what they are learning to something they already understand. This can help them grasp complex ideas more easily than younger learners.`nOn the other hand, adults may have less time to dedicate to learning. Responsibilities such as work and family can limit the hours available for practice. As a result, progress may appear slower, even if the quality of learning is high.`nExperts suggest that the best approach for adult learners is to focus on consistency rather than speed. Short, regular practice sessions are more effective than occasional long ones. In addition, creating a supportive environment and setting realistic goals can help maintain motivation over time.`nIn conclusion, although adults may face certain challenges when learning new skills, they also possess strengths that can lead to success. With the right strategies, learning can remain a rewarding experience at any age.`n`nQuestions`n31. What is the writer’s main point in the first paragraph?`nA. Children are better learners than adults in every wayB. Adults and children learn differently, but both have advantagesC. Adults should not try to learn new skillsD. Learning becomes impossible after a certain age`n`n32. Why do adults sometimes learn more slowly than children?`nA. They have less intelligenceB. They lack interest in learningC. They are afraid of making mistakesD. They prefer learning alone`n`n33. What helps adults learn more effectively according to the text?`nA. Having a clear reason for learningB. Competing with other learnersC. Avoiding difficult subjectsD. Studying for long hours every day`n`n34. What advantage do adults have because of their experience?`nA. They can memorise information fasterB. They need less practiceC. They can connect new ideas to what they already knowD. They understand everything immediately`n`n35. Why might adults seem to make slower progress?`nA. They are less capable than childrenB. They forget information quicklyC. They have limited time to practiseD. They choose difficult topics`n`n36. What does the writer suggest is the best way for adults to learn?`nA. Studying as quickly as possibleB. Practising regularly in short sessionsC. Learning only with teachersD. Avoiding challenging material`n`nAnswer Key`nB `nC `nA `nC `nC `nB`n' where id='6f873582-07bf-436a-9a25-492b748678c7';
update public.levels_partes set "Descripción"='Part 6: Gapped Text`nYou are going to read an article about developing new hobbies. Six sentences have been removed from the article. Choose from the sentences A–G the one which fits each gap (31–36). There is one extra sentence which you do not need to use.`n`nText`nDiscovering New Hobbies`nTrying out new hobbies can be an exciting way to spend your free time and learn new skills. Many people, however, find it difficult to get started because they are unsure of what they might enjoy.`n(31) ______Sometimes, the best approach is simply to experiment with different activities until you find something that interests you. You might discover a passion you never expected.`nIt is important not to feel discouraged if you do not enjoy something straight away.(32) ______Giving yourself time to improve can make the experience more enjoyable.`nAnother useful idea is to learn from others. Joining a club or taking a class can provide valuable guidance and support.(33) ______Being part of a group can also make the activity more social and motivating.`nIn addition, hobbies do not need to be expensive or complicated. Many enjoyable activities require very little equipment or preparation.(34) ______Simple hobbies such as reading, drawing or walking can be just as rewarding.`nIt is also worth considering how much time you can realistically dedicate to your new hobby.(35) ______Choosing something that fits into your routine will make it easier to continue.`nFinally, remember that hobbies are meant to be enjoyable.(36) ______The goal is to relax and have fun, not to create unnecessary pressure.`n`nSentences`nA This allows you to meet people who share similar interests.B You should avoid choosing activities that require too much time.C In fact, many skills take time and practice to develop.D There is no need to become an expert immediately.E Some people worry about failing before they even begin.F As a result, it is important to choose something simple at first.G It is not necessary to spend a lot of money to get started.`n`nAnswer Key`n31 E32 C33 A34 G35 B36 D`n' where id='b170ab5a-f54f-4cae-ac2e-bd194181cafe';
update public.levels_partes set "Descripción"='Part 7: Multiple Matching`nYou are going to read an article in which four people talk about their experiences of learning new skills. For questions 37–42, choose from the people (A–D). The people may be chosen more than once.`n`nWhich person…`n37 mentions feeling more confident after learning something new?38 found it difficult to stay motivated at the beginning?39 learned something mainly for professional reasons?40 enjoyed the social aspect of the experience?41 talks about improving slowly over time?42 mentions being surprised by their own ability?`n`nTexts`nA – Laura`nI decided to learn how to cook properly last year because I was tired of eating ready-made meals. At first, I found it quite challenging, especially because I made a lot of mistakes. However, I kept practising, and over time I noticed that my skills were improving little by little. Now I feel much more comfortable in the kitchen and even enjoy trying new recipes.`n`nB – Mark`nFor me, learning graphic design was something I needed for my job. My company expected me to develop these skills, so I didn’t really have a choice. Although I wasn’t very interested at first, I soon realised that I was actually quite good at it. That surprised me, and now I even use these skills for personal projects.`n`nC – Sofia`nI joined a photography class because I wanted to meet new people while doing something creative. The classes were fun, and I really enjoyed sharing ideas with others. It was great to be part of a group where everyone supported each other. As a result, I not only improved my photography but also made several good friends.`n`nD – James`nI started learning a new language a few months ago. In the beginning, I found it hard to stay motivated because progress seemed very slow. Sometimes I felt like giving up, but I decided to continue. Eventually, I began to understand more, and that gave me the confidence to keep going.`n`nAnswer Key`n37 D38 D39 B40 C41 A42 B`n' where id='bd41d80c-bcdd-4ac9-9c06-13c2ab8fab6e';
commit;
